From 58fe8fc6989fb179ba146caec82bd4cc674157eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: qiyinxi <qiyinxi@foxmail.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 13:07:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 22/60] whpx: optional local APIC emulation in the hypervisor
 (kernel-irqchip)

Port of upstream QEMU's WHPX "kernel-irqchip" support (faf20793b5af,
QEMU 6.0; target/i386/whpx/whpx-apic.c) to this 2.12 tree, with the later
upstream fixes that apply to the xAPIC mode:

 - 4fdbad6c4981: with the hypervisor's APIC the TPR is APIC state; CR8 is
   no longer read or written (it is now the last entry of the bulk
   register list and left out of it).
 - 9533cc6d3604: MSIs with vector 0 are dropped (only for fixed and
   lowest-priority delivery here, so that NMI/INIT MSIs still go through).
 - ca7a6add3bd2 / 3588d68bc8da: a PIC interrupt queued through
   WHvRegisterPendingEvent does not end a HLT inside the hypervisor, so
   the vCPU is taken out of it explicitly (also after queueing an NMI).
 - 6ef6a0f04e5e: INIT/SIPI are left to the hypervisor (no SIPI trap).

The local APIC ("whpx-apic") is a QEMU device only for its state: IPIs,
EOIs, the APIC timer, HLT and wait-for-SIPI stay inside the hypervisor.
The PIC and the IOAPIC stay in QEMU: IOAPIC and PCI MSI writes hit the
APIC MMIO window and become WHvRequestInterrupt(); EOIs of
level-triggered vectors come back as WHvRunVpExitReasonX64ApicEoi and are
broadcast to the IOAPIC (under the BQL, unlike upstream); PIC interrupts
are queued as ExtINT events once the interrupt window opens.

State for savevm/loadvm: the APIC registers travel in the existing
"apic" section (same layout as the QEMU APIC, so snapshots load in
either mode); the activity state (wait-for-SIPI) and the TSC deadline,
which also live in the hypervisor, travel in env->mp_state and
env->tsc_deadline of the "cpu" section. On reset application processors
are put in wait-for-SIPI explicitly. The current count of the APIC timer
is not carried: after loadvm the timer restarts from its initial count,
as with KVM's and AEHD's in-kernel APIC.

Off by default. ANDROID_EMU_WHPX_KERNEL_IRQCHIP=1 turns it on where the
host supports it, except on Windows 10 (no perfmon capability), where
upstream also keeps it off with a PIC; =2 turns it on there too;
"-machine kernel-irqchip=on" makes it required and "=off" disables it.
The guest does not see x2APIC in this mode.

The exit statistics count WHvRunVpExitReasonX64ApicEoi as "apic-eoi".
